    "value": "Here’s a summary of the current status for \"spring-boot-test-web-client\" or \"spring-boot-webtestclient\" in Spring Boot 3.4 and 3.5:\n\n- There is **no official module named \"spring-boot-test-web-client\"** in Spring Boot 3.x or 4.x as of now. You might be thinking of either `spring-boot-webtestclient` or simply using the `WebTestClient` for integration testing in servlet (WebMvc) or reactive (WebFlux) environments.\n\n- **WebTestClient** is supported in Spring Boot 3.4 and 3.5, and it is widely used for testing web endpoints—especially for reactive WebFlux applications but can also be used for MVC tests.\n\n- The test infrastructure around WebTestClient is enabled via the `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` annotation (or its older package), but after Spring Boot 3.5, there is a clearer distinction:  \n  - `spring-boot-webtestclient` is the dependency/module that provides `WebTestClient` support for server-side (controller) testing.\n  - `spring-boot-webclient-test` is more oriented toward testing REST client-side code (i.e., things you write with Spring's `WebClient`), not server-side controllers.\n\n- **Breaking changes \u0026 module split:**  \n  If you upgrade from Spring Boot 3.4/3.5 to 4.0, `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` and related test utilities move to the `spring-boot-webtestclient` module/package. If your project starts failing to compile after such an upgrade, it’s likely because you are missing the explicit dependency on `spring-boot-webtestclient`. You need to add this dependency, as the annotation is no longer included in the main starter-test module from 4.0 onward. This distinction is key if you’re writing reusable test annotations or using composite test setups【3:2†source】【3:4†source】.\n\n- **How to use (Spring Boot 3.4/3.5)?**\n  - In your `pom.xml` or `build.gradle`, just having `spring-boot-starter-test` and, if using WebFlux, `spring-boot-starter-webflux`, will bring in all the necessary test infrastructure.\n  - Typical usage:  \n    ```java\n    @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)\n    @AutoConfigureWebTestClient // make sure this is imported properly\n    public class MyControllerTest {\n        @Autowired\n        private WebTestClient webTestClient;\n    }\n    ```\n  - If you get errors about missing classes (e.g., `NoOpResponseErrorHandler`), it may be due to mixed or mismatched Spring Framework dependencies. Make sure all your Spring dependencies are compatible with your Spring Boot parent/version【3:1†source】.\n\n**Summary Table:**  \n\n| Feature/Module                    | Spring Boot 3.4/3.5                          | Spring Boot 4.0+                |\n|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| `spring-boot-webtestclient`       | Not a separate dependency; use starter-test  | Must explicitly add dependency  |\n| `spring-boot-webclient-test`      | Not relevant for controller tests            | Client-side REST testing only   |\n| `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient`     | In starter-test (import path may change)     | In spring-boot-webtestclient    |\n| WebTestClient usage (test server) | Supported                                    | Supported                       |\n\n**Action:**  \n- For Spring Boot 3.4/3.5, you **do not need** a separate `spring-boot-test-web-client` or `spring-boot-webtestclient` dependency: `spring-boot-starter-test` (and possibly `spring-boot-starter-webflux` for reactive apps) is enough.  \n- Always check your dependency tree for version mismatches if you face errors during upgrades or when enabling/disabling WebTestClient.\n\n**References:**  \n- Upgrading issues \u0026 solution with explicit module for WebTestClient: [StackOverflow - Spring Boot 4.x][3:2†source]\n- Testing docs \u0026 recommendations: [Spring Boot Docs][3:0†source]",
