    "value": "If you're looking for information on using Spring Boot's WebTestClient with version 3.5, here's what you need to know:\n\n### What is WebTestClient?\nWebTestClient is an HTTP client designed for testing Spring server applications. It allows you to make HTTP calls to your controllers as part of your tests, validating responses. It can be used with both Spring WebFlux (reactive) and Spring MVC (servlet) applications, and it works without launching a real web server by binding to your application context or individual controllers【3:0†source】【3:3†source】【3:8†source】.\n\n### Using WebTestClient with Spring Boot 3.5\n\n#### Dependencies\nFor Spring Boot 3.5, you typically need:\n```xml\n\u003cdependency\u003e\n    \u003cgroupId\u003eorg.springframework.boot\u003c/groupId\u003e\n    \u003cartifactId\u003espring-boot-starter-test\u003c/artifactId\u003e\n    \u003cscope\u003etest\u003c/scope\u003e\n\u003c/dependency\u003e\n\u003cdependency\u003e\n    \u003cgroupId\u003eorg.springframework.boot\u003c/groupId\u003e\n    \u003cartifactId\u003espring-boot-starter-webflux\u003c/artifactId\u003e\n    \u003cscope\u003etest\u003c/scope\u003e \u003c!-- or implementatino if you use Webflux in the app --\u003e\n\u003c/dependency\u003e\n```\n- The `spring-boot-starter-test` starter brings in all essential test dependencies, including `spring-test` which contains WebTestClient.\n- The `spring-boot-starter-webflux` is required for reactive support.\n\n#### Usage Example\n```java\n@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)\nclass MyControllerTest {\n    @Autowired\n    private WebTestClient webTestClient;\n\n    @Test\n    void myTest() {\n        webTestClient.get().uri(\"/api/endpoint\")\n            .exchange()\n            .expectStatus().isOk();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### Changes in Spring Boot 3.5\n- The overall setup is nearly the same as previous 3.x releases.\n- Make sure all your dependencies (especially anything `spring-web*` or `spring-test`) are managed by Spring Boot's BOM to avoid version mismatches, as dependency management moved away from the `spring-boot-parent` POM since 3.5【3:5†source】【3:6†source】.\n- There are release notes and migration considerations, especially if moving from a previous 3.x version. E.g., no more `spring-boot-parent` module; you must manage dependencies yourself if you're not using the parent POM.\n\n#### Common Pitfalls\n- Make sure your test dependencies for webflux or webmvc all match the boot version.\n- If you get missing classes like `NoOpResponseErrorHandler`, you might need to bump your `spring-web` or related dependencies to what is expected by Boot 3.5【3:6†source】.\n- In Boot 4.x, note that auto-configuration for WebTestClient has moved to a dedicated module (`spring-boot-webtestclient`), so you need that dependency directly. For 3.5, you don't need to worry about that yet【3:2†source】.\n\n### Official Reference\n- See [Spring Boot 3.5 Release Notes](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.5-Release-Notes) for details on what's changed【3:5†source】.\n- General usage is well documented in [the official Spring docs](https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/testing/webtestclient.html) and on Baeldung【3:0†source】【3:8†source】.\n\nIf you need Gradle-style dependencies or encounter a specific error, please provide your build system and the error message for tailored help!",
